analyticSpread -- Compute the analytic spread of a module or ideal
******************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        analyticSpread M
        analyticSpread(M,f)
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, or an *note ideal:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, an optional
        element, which is a non-zerodivisor such that $M[f^{-1}]$ is a free
        module when $M$ is a module, an element in $M$ when $M$ is an ideal
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note BasisElementLimit:
        minimalReduction_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmBasisElementLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value infinity, Bound the number of Groebner basis elements to
        compute in the saturation step
      * *note DegreeLimit: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value {}, Bound the degrees considered in the
        saturation step. Defaults to infinity
      * *note MinimalGenerators:
        reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMinimalGenerators=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value true, Whether the saturation step returns minimal
        generators
      * *note PairLimit: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmPairLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value infinity, Bound the number of s-pairs considered in
        the saturation step
      * *note Strategy: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, Choose a strategy for the saturation step
  * Outputs:
      * an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the analytic spread of a module or
        an ideal $M$

Description
===========

The analytic spread of a module is the dimension of its special fiber ring.
When $I$ is an ideal (and more generally, with the right definitions) the
analytic spread of $I$ is the smallest number of generators of an ideal $J$
such that $I$ is integral over $J$. See for example the book Integral closure
of ideals, rings, and modules. London Mathematical Society Lecture Note Series,
336. Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 2006, by Craig Huneke and Irena
Swanson.

