multiplicity -- Compute the Hilbert-Samuel multiplicity of an ideal
*******************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        multiplicity I
        multiplicity(I,f)
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, 
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, optional argument,
        if given it should be a non-zero divisor in the ideal I
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note BasisElementLimit:
        minimalReduction_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmBasisElementLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value infinity, Bound the number of Groebner basis elements to
        compute in the saturation step
      * *note DegreeLimit: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value {}, Bound the degrees considered in the
        saturation step. Defaults to infinity
      * *note MinimalGenerators:
        reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMinimalGenerators=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value true, Whether the saturation step returns minimal
        generators
      * *note PairLimit: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmPairLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value infinity, Bound the number of s-pairs considered in
        the saturation step
      * *note Strategy: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, Choose a strategy for the saturation step
      * *note Variable: intersectInP_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmBasisElementLimit=>_pd_pd_pd
        _rp, => ..., default value "w", Option for intersectInP
  * Outputs:
      * an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the normalized leading coefficient
        of the Hilbert-Samuel polynomial of $I$

Description
===========

Given an ideal $I\subset{} R$, ``multiplicity I'' returns the degree of the
normal cone of $I$.  When $R/I$ has finite length this is the sum of the Samuel
multiplicities of $I$ at the various localizations of $R$. When $I$ is
generated by a complete intersection, this is the length of the ring $R/I$ but
in general it is greater. For example,

+-------------------------------+
|i1 : R=ZZ/101[x,y]             |
|                               |
|o1 = R                         |
|                               |
|o1 : PolynomialRing            |
+-------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(x^3, x^2*y, y^3)|
|                               |
|             3   2    3        |
|o2 = ideal (x , x y, y )       |
|                               |
|o2 : Ideal of R                |
+-------------------------------+
|i3 : multiplicity I            |
|                               |
|o3 = 9                         |
+-------------------------------+
|i4 : degree I                  |
|                               |
|o4 = 7                         |
+-------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The normal cone is computed using the Rees algebra, thus may be slow.

Ways to use multiplicity:
=========================

  * "multiplicity(Ideal)"
  * "multiplicity(Ideal,RingElement)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note multiplicity: multiplicity, is a *note method function with
options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

