reesIdeal -- Compute the defining ideal of the Rees Algebra
***********************************************************

  * Usage: 
        reesIdeal M
        reesIdeal(M,f)
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, or an *note ideal:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal, of a quotient polynomial ring $R$
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, any
        non-zerodivisor in ideal or the first Fitting ideal of the module.
        Optional
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note BasisElementLimit:
        minimalReduction_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmBasisElementLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value infinity, Bound the number of Groebner basis elements to
        compute in the saturation step
      * *note DegreeLimit: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value {}, Bound the degrees considered in the
        saturation step. Defaults to infinity
      * Jacobian => ..., default value false
      * *note MinimalGenerators:
        reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMinimalGenerators=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value true, Whether the saturation step returns minimal
        generators
      * *note PairLimit: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmPairLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value infinity, Bound the number of s-pairs considered in
        the saturation step
      * *note Strategy: reesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, Choose a strategy for the saturation step
      * Trim => ..., default value true
      * *note Variable: symmetricKernel_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVariable=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value "w", Choose name for variables in the created
        ring
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, defining the Rees algebra of M

Description
===========

This routine gives the user a choice between two methods for finding the
defining ideal of the Rees algebra of an ideal or module $M$ over a ring $R$:
The command reesIdeal(M) computes a versal embedding $g: M\to G$ and a
surjection $f: F\to M$ and returns the result of symmetricKernel(gf).

When M is an ideal (the usual case) or in characteristic 0, the same ideal can
be computed by an alternate method that is often faster. If the user knows a
non-zerodivisor $a\in{} R$ such that $M[a^{-1}$ is a free module (for example,
when M is an ideal, any non-zerodivisor $a \in{} M$ then it is often much
faster to compute reesIdeal(M,a) which computes the saturation of the defining
ideal of the symmetric algebra of M with respect to a. This gives the correct
answer even under the slightly weaker hypothesis that $M[a^{-1}]$ is of linear
type. (See also *note isLinearType: isLinearType,.)



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : kk = ZZ/101;                                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S=kk[x_0..x_4];                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : i= trim monomialCurveIdeal(S,{2,3,5,6})                                 |
|                                                                             |
|                          2                       3      2     2      2   2  |
|o3 = ideal (x x  - x x , x  - x x , x x  - x x , x  - x x , x x  - x x , x x |
|             2 3    1 4   2    0 4   1 2    0 3   3    2 4   1 3    0 4   1 3|
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|                3    2                                                       |
|     - x x x , x  - x x )                                                    |
|        0 2 4   1    0 4                                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : Ideal of S                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : time V1 = reesIdeal i;                                                  |
| -- used 0.0361089s (cpu); 0.034095s (thread); 0s (gc)                       |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : Ideal of S[w ..w ]                                                      |
|                 0   6                                                       |
